		<div class="post" id="post-3921077">
		<div class="long">
			<div class="head">
									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,3921077)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-3921077">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/eskobar" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1173523); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1173523&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608973859" alt="Eskobar" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1173523)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/eskobar" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1173523); return false;" >Eskobar</a></span> <span class="odate time_1534907521 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">22 Aug 2018 03:12</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3921077">
									

<p>Gosh, I forgot how hard it was to finally hit the &quot;Save&quot; button on one of these. Anyway, it's my new work of fiction, the fictional story that's here. Everything that happens here is a work of fiction. Keep that in mind.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">.</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Well,&nbsp;sort&nbsp;of.</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I waffled back and forth on posting this.</p>
<p>When I was still debating on whether I was going to be back around the wiki, I kept finding myself feeling like I needed to explain myself. Like I was going to be asked &quot;what the hell happened, dipshit?&quot; So I wrote&#8230;well, actually, I wrote a couple of different versions of this story. Trying to piece it together for myself, so I could have it straight when the inevitable interrogations came my way. I couldn't write or work on anything until I felt like I had an adequate explanation. There was a lot to explain.</p>
<p>It never once happened that way. Not once in the last two months or so that I've been bebopping around have I caught static about The Death and Return of Eskobar. Which is dope. Good community we have around here. Nice foundations, so to speak. Solid.</p>
<p>I kept coming back to this story, getting some critique from people who might not want to be associated with this, streamlining the garbage throughout the piece. (I apparently have a love for dialogue tags that exceeds their usefulness. And something about ellipses and spaces that I don't do right. I'll work on that.) It didn't feel right to post it, but it didn't feel right to shelve it, either.</p>
<p>I decided to post it on the wiki for three reasons. First of all, the work lives and dies by the community, ultimately. If it shouldn't be here, it won't be, regardless of my opinion. That's awesome. Let's see more of that. Second of all, I really developed a fondness for the Department of Personnel Integrity, which started out as a throwaway concept and really evolved as the writing process continued. I'm planning to use them in future stuff, and this is as much their story as it is mine.</p>
<p>And third of all, it feels true, as true as any one person's perspective on the past can be. There's a sensation I get when I write something that's <em>true</em>, in a deep, beyond-the-brain sense. The beginnings of tears. A loss of breath. A flutter around the heart. I don't know if what I'm describing will feel real to anyone else, but rereading it here a few minutes ago, it felt <em>beyond</em> real. It's sure as hell not flattering. It's embarrassing as hell to expose myself, such as it were, in this public of a way. Nothing about this story makes me look good.</p>
<p>Several names are changed. Several names aren't. Some people will know what's happening here. Most people won't. It doesn't matter. This doesn't exist to relitigate the past, or call for action. Any concerns I may have had in 2015 have demonstrably not come to pass. The sky didn't fall. The ship is still afloat.</p>
<p>If it sucks, downvote it. If it don't, don't. I fucking love you people.</p>
<p>The beginnings of tears. A loss of breath. A flutter around the heart.</p>
<p>&#8212;Dave<br />
21 August 2018</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

